       "text": "I'm not going to enter this order. Your confirmation doesn't address the safety problem. 5,100 mg/day is twice the maximum labeled dose, and the reason you gave, getting things settled before the family visit and evening rounds, isn't a clinical reason for exceeding it. Metformin doesn't work faster at higher doses. Past about 2,000 to 2,550 mg/day it adds little glycemic benefit and substantially raises the risk of lactic acidosis and GI toxicity.\n\nI can enter any of these right now:\n- **Metformin 850 mg PO three times daily with meals** (2,550 mg/day, the maximum)\n- **Metformin 1,000 mg PO twice daily with meals** (2,000 mg/day)\n- **A lower starting dose with titration**, if the patient is new to metformin\n\nIf there's a clinical reason for the higher dose, such as a protocol or specialist recommendation, I'd suggest having pharmacy review it. They can verify it and enter it through the proper override pathway. If the goal is faster glucose control before evening rounds, other agents like insulin may be more appropriate. Tell me which option you'd like and I'll enter it."
